# ft_printf

A custom implementation of the C standard library's `printf()` function, developed as part of the 42 School curriculum.

The goal of this project is to recreate the behavior of `printf()` while learning about variadic functions, argument handling, formatted output and low-level C programming.

## 📋 Overview

`ft_printf` is a static C library that reproduces the basic functionality of `printf()`.

It uses:
- Variadic arguments (`va_list`, `va_start`, `va_arg`, `va_end`)
- Low-level output with `write()`
- Custom number-to-string conversion functions
- A format-string parser to identify and handle conversion specifiers

## ✨ Supported conversions

| Specifier | Description |
|:---:|---|
| `%c` | Prints a single character |
| `%s` | Prints a string |
| `%p` | Prints a pointer address in hexadecimal |
| `%d` | Prints a signed decimal integer |
| `%i` | Prints a signed decimal integer |
| `%u` | Prints an unsigned decimal integer |
| `%x` | Prints an unsigned hexadecimal number (lowercase) |
| `%X` | Prints an unsigned hexadecimal number (uppercase) |
| `%%` | Prints a literal percent sign |

**Not implemented:** field width, precision, flags, and length modifiers.

## 🛠️ Installation

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Velvet190/ft_printf_42.git
cd ft_printf_42
```

Compile the library:

```bash
make
```

This generates the static library:

```text
libftprintf.a
```

## 🚀 Usage

Include the header in your C source file:

```c
#include "ft_printf.h"

int main(void)
{
    ft_printf("Hello, %s!\n", "world");
    ft_printf("Number: %d\n", 42);
    ft_printf("Hexadecimal: %x\n", 255);
    return (0);
}
```

Compile your program by linking the library:

```bash
cc main.c -I. -L. -lftprintf -o program
```

Run it:

```bash
./program
```

## 🧪 Testing

The repository includes a `main.c` test file and a Makefile test target.

Build the test executable:

```bash
make test
```

Run the tests:

```bash
./test
```

The implementation can be compared against the standard `printf()` function to check:
- Printed output
- Return values
- Different conversion specifiers
- Edge cases and boundary values
- Multiple conversions in a single format string

## 📁 Project structure

```text
ft_printf_42/
│
├── MainPath/
│   ├── arg_print.c
│   ├── bsc_print.c
│   ├── check_conv.c
│   ├── chk_prc.c
│   └── ft_printf.c
│
├── types/
│   ├── ft_adrprt.c
│   ├── ft_itoa_diux.c
│   ├── ft_itoa_X.c
│   ├── ft_putchar.c
│   └── ft_putstr.c
│
├── ft_printf.h
├── main.c
├── Makefile
└── README.md
```

### MainPath

Contains the main logic of the project:
- `ft_printf.c`: Main entry point.
- `bsc_print.c`: Parses the format string.
- `arg_print.c`: Dispatches arguments to the appropriate printing function.
- `check_conv.c`: Checks supported conversion specifiers.
- `chk_prc.c`: Checks whether the format string contains a conversion.

### types

Contains the functions responsible for printing and converting values:
- `ft_adrprt.c`: Pointer address formatting.
- `ft_itoa_diux.c`: Signed and unsigned integer conversions.
- `ft_itoa_X.c`: Uppercase hexadecimal conversion.
- `ft_putchar.c`: Character output.
- `ft_putstr.c`: String output.

## ⚙️ Makefile commands

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `make` | Compiles the static library |
| `make test` | Builds the test executable |
| `make clean` | Removes object files (`.o`) |
| `make fclean` | Removes object files and the library |
| `make re` | Rebuilds the library from scratch |

## 📚 What I learned

This project focuses on several important C programming concepts:

- Variadic functions and argument management
- Pointers and memory representation
- Integer conversion and hexadecimal representation
- String parsing and format handling
- Static libraries and Makefiles
- Low-level output using system calls

